  <section class="brx-sec brx-tint" id="cost">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>How much do new radiators cost in Liverpool?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">A new radiator supplied and fitted costs from £245 including VAT. Five radiators supplied and fitted cost from £1,195.</p>
      <div class="brx-table-wrap">
        <table class="brx-table">
          <thead><tr><th>Job</th><th>Price inc VAT</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><strong>New radiator, supplied and fitted</strong></td><td>From £245</td><td>Standard double panel, same spot</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Five radiators, supplied and fitted</strong></td><td>From £1,195</td><td>Usually done in one day</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Fit your own radiator</strong></td><td>From £95</td><td>Same spot, no new pipework</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Designer or column radiator, fit only</strong></td><td>From £250</td><td>Heavier, often wall-mounted brackets</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Move a radiator</strong></td><td>From £225</td><td>Pipework moved to a new wall</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>New radiator in a new position</strong></td><td>From £325</td><td>New pipework from the circuit</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>TRV fitted</strong></td><td>£65 each, or up to 6 for £295</td><td>Set the heat room by room</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Radiator repairs and leaks</strong></td><td>First hour £120</td><td>£150 evenings and weekends, £180 bank holidays</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <h3>What is included</h3>
      <ul class="sg-ticks">
        <li>The new radiator and all fittings (supply and fit jobs)</li>
        <li>Old radiator taken off and taken away</li>
        <li>Dust sheets down and the area left clean</li>
        <li>Inhibitor checked and topped up</li>
        <li>System refilled, bled and balanced so every room heats evenly</li>
        <li>A 12-month workmanship guarantee</li>
      </ul>
      <p>There is no call-out fee. A straight swap can often be priced on the phone. For moves, new positions and designer radiators, we look first and give you a fixed written price.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="brx-sec" id="types">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>Which radiator is right for me?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">For most rooms, a double panel radiator gives the most heat for the money. Column and designer radiators are about the look, so check their heat output first.</p>
      <div class="sg-cols">
        <div class="sg-box">
          <h3>Panel radiators</h3>
          <p>The most common type in Liverpool homes. Double panel radiators give lots of heat for their size. We fit Stelrad, Quinn and Myson. This is what our £245 price covers.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="sg-box">
          <h3>Column radiators</h3>
          <p>They suit Victorian and Edwardian homes. We fit two, three and four-column styles, up to 1800mm tall for high ceilings. Carron and Stelrad Column are popular.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="sg-box">
          <h3>Designer radiators</h3>
          <p>Vertical radiators help where wall space is tight. We fit brands like Reina, Hudson Reed and Bisque. Many give less heat than a panel radiator, so size them with care.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="sg-box">
          <h3>Towel rails</h3>
          <p>In chrome, white or anthracite. A dual-fuel rail can run on its own in summer, using an electric element. That needs a safe electrical supply. Our partner firm <a href="https://www.idealelectricalsolutions.co.uk/">Ideal Electrical Solutions</a> can fit it.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p style="margin-top:1rem;">We also fit low surface temperature radiators. Their surface stays cool enough to touch, so they suit rooms used by young children or older people.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="brx-sec brx-tint" id="size">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>What size radiator do I need?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">The right size depends on how much heat the room loses. We work it out for you, and pick a radiator with enough output.</p>
      <p>Heat output is shown in watts or BTU. To size a radiator, we look at:</p>
      <ul class="brx-list">
        <li>The size of the room and the height of the ceiling</li>
        <li>How many windows it has, and whether they are double glazed</li>
        <li>Solid or cavity walls, and how many walls face outside</li>
        <li>What is above and below the room</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Too small, and the room never warms up. Too big, and the room overheats and wastes gas. A cold front room in an old terrace is often down to a small radiator under a big bay window.</p>
      <div class="brx-note"><p><strong>Buying online?</strong> Compare the output figure, not the size. Two radiators the same width can give very different heat. A slim designer radiator may give much less than a double panel one.</p></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="brx-sec" id="how">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>How does a radiator installation work?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">A straight swap takes 1 to 2 hours. Five radiators are usually done in one day.</p>
      <ol class="sg-steps">
        <li><strong>Call for a price.</strong> Tell us the room and the radiator. A straight swap can often be priced on the phone. For moves or new positions, we visit first.</li>
        <li><strong>Fixed written price.</strong> It covers the radiator, fittings, labour and balancing. No add-ons on the day.</li>
        <li><strong>Drain and remove (30 minutes).</strong> We close the valves and drain just that radiator where we can. If the valves will not hold, we drain the system.</li>
        <li><strong>Fit the new radiator (30 to 60 minutes).</strong> New brackets go up and the radiator is connected. We protect floors with dust sheets.</li>
        <li><strong>Refill and balance (30 minutes).</strong> We top up the inhibitor, refill and bleed. Then we balance the system so every room heats evenly.</li>
        <li><strong>Old radiator taken away.</strong> We tidy up before we leave.</li>
      </ol>
      <div class="brx-cta">
        <p><strong>Need new radiators fitted?</strong> Call for a fixed price, from £245 including VAT.</p>
        <a href={TEL} class="brx-btn">Call {PHONE}</a>
      </div>
      <h3>Should I power flush before fitting new radiators?</h3>
      <p>If your radiators are cold at the bottom, or bleed black water, yes. Sludge in the system will soon clog new radiators. If your radiators heat well and the water is clear, there is no need. We check this when we price the job. See our <a href="/power-flushing-liverpool">power flushing prices</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="brx-sec brx-tint" id="add-move">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>Can you add or move a radiator?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">Yes. Moving a radiator is from £225. A new radiator in a room without one is from £325.</p>
      <div class="sg-cols">
        <div class="sg-box">
          <h3>Adding a radiator</h3>
          <p>Common for extensions, loft rooms, garage conversions and conservatories. We run new pipes from the nearest part of the circuit. We fit the radiator with a TRV, then balance the system. It takes about 2 to 4 hours.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="sg-box">
          <h3>Moving a radiator</h3>
          <p>Common for new kitchens, built-in furniture and new room layouts. We drain the system, move the pipes, fit the radiator on the new wall and refill. Most moves take 3 to 4 hours.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p style="margin-top:1rem;">We check the spot first. It needs to be near the pipes, with enough wall space. If your chosen spot will not work, we tell you and suggest the next best place.</p>
      <p>Adding a lot of radiators? Your boiler must be big enough to heat them. For a full new system, see <a href="/central-heating-installation-liverpool">central heating installation</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="brx-sec" id="repairs">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>Radiator repairs in Liverpool: how much do they cost?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">Radiator repairs are charged by time. The first hour is £120 on weekdays from 9am to 4pm. There is no call-out fee.</p>
      <div class="brx-table-wrap">
        <table class="brx-table">
          <thead><tr><th>When</th><th>First hour inc VAT</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><strong>Weekdays 9am to 4pm</strong></td><td>£120</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Evenings, nights and weekends</strong></td><td>£150</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Bank holidays</strong></td><td>£180</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>Most small repairs are done inside the first hour. If it needs more time, we tell you the price before we carry on.</p>
      <h3>Common radiator problems we fix</h3>
      <ul class="sg-ticks">
        <li><strong>Leaking valve.</strong> Often a loose gland nut or a worn valve. We tighten or replace it.</li>
        <li><strong>Leaking pipe under a radiator.</strong> We cut out the bad section and repair it.</li>
        <li><strong>Pinhole in the radiator.</strong> The radiator has rusted through. It needs replacing, not repairing.</li>
        <li><strong>Radiator stone cold.</strong> Often a stuck TRV pin or a closed valve. Usually a quick fix.</li>
        <li><strong>Noisy radiators.</strong> Banging or gurgling can mean air, sludge or a pump fault. We find the cause first.</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="brx-danger"><p><strong>Radiator leaking now?</strong> Put a bowl and towels under it. Close the valves on both ends, turning them clockwise. If it will not stop, turn the water off at the stopcock and call {PHONE}.</p></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="brx-sec brx-tint" id="older-homes">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>Radiators in older Liverpool homes</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">Victorian and Edwardian homes need more care with pipe routes and sizing. We plan both at the first visit.</p>
      <p>Older homes in Aigburth, Toxteth, Wavertree and Anfield have solid brick walls. Pipes cannot be hidden inside them. Where there is a suspended timber floor, we run pipes underneath. Where the floor is solid, we use neat boxing on the wall. We keep skirting and coving as they were.</p>
      <p>Column radiators suit period rooms. But double panel radiators give more heat for the price. Many people mix them: column in the front room, panels in bedrooms and the kitchen. For more on heating older homes, read our <a href="/blog/central-heating-liverpool-victorian-terrace">guide to heating a Victorian terrace</a>.</p>
      <h3>TRVs</h3>
      <p>A TRV on each radiator lets you heat each room as you need. Turn the spare room down and the living room up. We usually leave one radiator without a TRV, often in the hall, so water can always flow. The <a href="https://energysavingtrust.org.uk/advice/thermostats-and-heating-controls/">Energy Saving Trust</a> has more advice on heating controls.</p>
      <h3>Who does the work?</h3>
      <p>Our heating engineers are part of our team led by James. All radiator work on the heating circuit is done by our Gas Safe registered engineers. You can check any engineer on the <a href="https://www.gassaferegister.co.uk">Gas Safe Register</a>. We carry £2 million public liability insurance.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</ServiceGuide>
